  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nQuiet weather ahead for the next several days. A weak front settles \ninto the area tonight into Monday morning. This should bring lighter \nwinds and a bit cooler low temperatures, mainly from the upper 30s \nto lower 40s. With the light winds and some weak surface convergence \nalong the boundary, some patchy fog will be possible. For now the \nfog appears as though it will mainly be in low lying locations.\n\nMonday should prove the be a fairly mild day with winds generally \nbelow 15 mph. Highs mostly mid 60s.\n\nStronger southerly flow will be in place on Tuesday which could \nbring some elevated fire weather concerns as temperatures climb into \nthe 70s. A weak boundary settle south on Wednesday, with winds \nexpected to be much weaker than Tuesday. With some heating ahead of \nthe incoming front, highs should climb into the mid 70s in most \nlocations with an 80 degree reading not out of the question.\n\nStronger troughing develops to the west Thursday into Friday which \nwill bring an increase in southerly flow to the area, with some \nelevated fire weather concerns once again. Highs both days will be \nin the 70s.\n\nRain chances increase Friday into Saturday morning as this trough \nspreads on the Northern Plains. For now rainfall amounts look \nfairly light with the 50th percentile from the EC/Canadian and GFS \nensemble output all less than a quarter of an inch, with the \nCanadian and EC Ensemble generally less than a tenth of an inch. Low \ntemperatures on Friday and Saturday mornings will be very mild with \nstronger southerly flow in place. Lows both mornings will be just a \ntouch below the seasonal normals for high temperatures. \n\nSaturday and Sunday will see cooler temperatures but for now the air \nmass behind this system is not looking too cold, so anticipating \nhighs in the 60s both days.",
